new file mode 100644
index 00000000..070084ab
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.seikika/STANDARD.md
@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+# S1: no direct ORM queries on person tables
+
+**The standard.** From [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md): never add new direct ORM queries like
+`Person.objects.filter(...)` or `PersonDistinctId.objects.filter(...)`. The team extends this to the
+group tables (`Group`, `GroupTypeMapping`), see *History*. Use the existing routed helpers
+in `posthog/models/person/util.py`, or add a new one there following the established pattern.
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **Violation** | Each occurrence of `Person.objects`, `PersonDistinctId.objects`, `CohortPeople.objects`, `Group.objects` or `GroupTypeMapping.objects` in a Python file (comments and strings included: the check is textual) |
+| **Exempt** | Test files: anything under a `test/` or `tests/` directory, `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, `conftest.py`. The routed helpers: `posthog/models/person/util.py`, `posthog/models/group_type_mapping.py`. The model module `posthog/models/person/person.py`: its managers back the routed helpers |
+| **Enforced by** | A ratchet: the violations recorded in `.seikika/results.json` may only go down. A new file with violations, or more violations in a file than recorded, fails CI (`.github/workflows/seikika-standards.yml`) |
+| **Goal** | Zero. Progress is the `person-orm-zero` campaign |
+
+It is a [Seikika](https://github.com/seikika/seikika) repo portal: `seikika report` scores every
+Python file against the `person-orm` scorecard in `.seikika/seikika.yaml`, and `.seikika/s1.sh` shapes
+that into the commands below.
+
+## Commands
+
+Run from the repository root, with `seikika` (0.1.1) and `jq` on `PATH`. Each takes about 40 s
+(it reads every Python file).
+
+| What | Command | Output / exit code |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Report** | `.seikika/s1.sh report` | JSON `[{"file": "<path>", "count": <n>}, ...]`, most violations first |
+| **Gate** | `.seikika/s1.sh gate` | Exit 1 on a new file with violations or a file with more than recorded; exit 0 otherwise. Says when violations went down |
+| **Campaign** | `.seikika/s1.sh campaign` | JSON: `baseline` (count when the campaign started), `current`, `percentDone`, and the same per file |
+| Update the baseline | `.seikika/s1.sh update` | Rewrites `.seikika/results.json`; refuses if anything regressed |
+| Full scorecard (Markdown or HTML) | `seikika report .seikika` / `seikika report .seikika -o html --out s1.html` | Every file's level with evidence for failing ones; the HTML page also shows the campaign |
+
+## After fixing violations
+
+1. Run `.seikika/s1.sh gate`. It prints `✓ better … evidence shrank 10 → 9` for each improved file and
+   `S1 violations went down. Tighten the baseline`.
+2. Run `.seikika/s1.sh update` and commit `.seikika/results.json` with the fix.
+
+`results.json` is generated: never hand-edit or hand-merge it. On a merge conflict, take the target
+branch's copy and rerun `.seikika/s1.sh update`. Review its diff: `update` also records any new file's
+violations as allowed (Seikika treats a new file as a change, not a regression), so run `gate` first
+and only update on a tree that passes it.
+
+The campaign's starting point is `.seikika/s1-campaign-start.json` (102 violations in 37 files on
+2026-11-21, recorded after the Group/GroupTypeMapping change, see *History*). Don't change it during
+the campaign. To start a new campaign, run
+`.seikika/s1.sh campaign-start` and update the `start` date of `person-orm-zero` in `seikika.yaml`.
+
+## Changing the standard
+
+All of it lives in `.seikika/seikika.yaml`; after any change run `seikika validate .seikika`, then
+`.seikika/s1.sh update`, and commit both files (plus this one) in one change that the owning team reviews.
+
+- **Add a model** (e.g. `PersonOverride`): add it to the alternation in the connector's `counts.personOrm`
+  regex, `'\b(?:Person|PersonDistinctId|CohortPeople|Group|GroupTypeMapping|PersonOverride)\.objects\b'`,
+  to the rule's `evidence` label and `fix`, the campaign `description`, the gate message in `s1.sh`,
+  and the *Violation* row above. Existing uses then count as regressions (a file that passed now
+  fails, a violating file's count grows), so `s1.sh update` refuses. To record them, reset the
+  baseline: `rm .seikika/results.json && .seikika/s1.sh update`, run `gate` (it must pass) and review
+  the diff: it must change only files that use the new model, or that you exempted. Then decide the
+  campaign's starting point (see *History*). New uses after the change fail the gate.
+- **Add an exempt file** (a new routed helper): add its path to the `entity.path in [...]` list in the
+  `File` kind's `s1Exempt` field (put the reason in the field's `description`: a `#` line inside the
+  `computed` block would become part of the CEL expression), and to the *Exempt* row above. The gate
+  reports the file as `✓ better … now passes`; `s1.sh update` records it.
+- **Add an exempt pattern** (e.g. a directory): add a `|| entity.path.matches('<regex>')` clause to
+  `s1Exempt`. Exempt files are `skipped` by the scorecard, so they are neither gated nor counted.
+- **Scope**: the connector reads `files: ["**/*.py"]` (8,947 files here, the same as `git ls-files '*.py'`).
+
+## Limits
+
+- One evidence item is generated per occurrence, up to 200 per file (a CEL list literal, because
+  Seikika captures are de-duplicated). A file with more than 200 occurrences would be counted as 200.
+- The baseline records every Python file (about 9,000 entries, ~1 MB), because Seikika records every
+  scored entity. A new file without violations does not fail the gate, but makes `results.json`
+  out of date; refresh it with `.seikika/s1.sh update` whenever convenient.
+
+## History
+
+**2026-11-21: group tables added, the person model module exempted.** `Group.objects` and
+`GroupTypeMapping.objects` became violations, and `posthog/models/person/person.py` was exempted (the
+model module; its managers back the routed helpers). Because the update refuses to record the new
+models' existing uses, the baseline was reset (deleted and regenerated). Net effect on the Report:
+80 violations in 28 files became 102 in 37: +32 new-model uses (10 newly violating files, 4 files
+with more), and -10 from exempting `person.py`.
+
+The campaign was restarted from this state (`.seikika/s1-campaign-start.json` rewritten with
+`s1.sh campaign-start`; the `start` date in `seikika.yaml` stays 2026-11-21). Why: the campaign had
+started the same day and nothing was fixed yet (80 of 80 left), so nothing was lost. Keeping the old
+starting point would have mixed two definitions: `campaign` compares today's total (new models
+included, `person.py` excluded) with the old start total, so it would have shown -27.5 % done, and
+later fixes to group queries would have counted against a baseline that never included them. The
+old starting point was 80 violations in 28 files. Note that `posthog/models/group/util.py` (5) and
+`posthog/models/group/group.py` (1) now count as violations: if they are the routed helpers for
+groups, exempt them like `person/util.py` (a decision for the owning team).
